# 17. Definition of done

The application is complete only when all of the following are true:

- [ ] The app is named UP OR DOWN throughout.
- [ ] The interface feels like a deliberately designed iOS game.
- [ ] Play, Stats and Settings work.
- [ ] Users can choose categories and difficulty.
- [ ] Higher/Lower comparisons work correctly.
- [ ] Scores, streaks, records and results are accurate.
- [ ] Classic mode works.
- [ ] 60-Second Sprint works.
- [ ] Daily Challenge works offline and uses deterministic sequencing.
- [ ] The application includes at least 10,000 distinct, source-backed numerical observations.
- [ ] There are at least 20 playable categories or subcategories across six or more groups.
- [ ] Data volume is measured from the actual bundled records.
- [ ] Every published dataset has appropriate provenance and licence information.
- [ ] No fabricated facts have been introduced to meet the target.
- [ ] No incompatible facts can be compared.
- [ ] Units, periods, missing values and ties are handled correctly.
- [ ] Data validation and coverage-report commands work.
- [ ] The PWA installs and works on iOS Safari.
- [ ] The application runs from a GitHub Pages subpath.
- [ ] It can load and play offline after the initial successful load.
- [ ] Data, settings and statistics persist safely.
- [ ] Light and dark themes work.
- [ ] Accessibility and reduced-motion requirements are respected.
- [ ] Unit tests, end-to-end tests, type checks and production builds pass.
- [ ] There are no outstanding critical gameplay, data-integrity or console errors.
- [ ] Final screenshots have been reviewed rather than merely generated.
- [ ] Documentation accurately describes what was built.

If any item fails, investigate it and fix it before declaring completion. Clearly document any limitation that genuinely cannot be resolved.

